Having said that&nbsp;"when you are not physically with people" I think our&nbsp;ability to communicate has actually decreased.&nbsp;

00:14:14.218 --> 00:14:26.241
Because we lose those skills when we become&nbsp;so used to contacting people exclusively via&nbsp;technology you do lose that capacity to have&nbsp;real meaningful connections face to face.

00:14:26.241 --> 00:14:33.952
So I&nbsp;think that that's definitely something a way that&nbsp;our communication has actually, um reduced.&nbsp;

00:14:34.468 --> 00:14:38.212
Yeah.&nbsp;And can body language help you communicate better?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:45.187
Yes, definitely. Um, and this sort of flows on from&nbsp;that idea about face-to-face communication.

00:14:45.187 --> 00:14:54.655
Um, because when obviously, when you contact people through a&nbsp;phone or through a computer, it's very difficult to&nbsp;sense their body language.&nbsp;

00:14:54.655 --> 00:15:03.950
Um, so being in person and&nbsp;being able to read people's body language and, you&nbsp;know, get vibes, I guess, from people, you could&nbsp;say.

00:15:03.950 --> 00:15:08.400
 It's really, really important it's really&nbsp;difficult to achieve that through technology.&nbsp;

00:15:08.400 --> 00:15:13.880
And&nbsp;if you were having a difficult conversation with&nbsp;someone, would you prefer to have it over the phone&nbsp;or in person. &nbsp;

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:22.195
I would always prefer to have those&nbsp;sorts of conversations in person because I think&nbsp;it really is important to sort of connect with the&nbsp;other person.

00:15:22.195 --> 00:15:31.440
In terms of seeing like visually how&nbsp;they're reacting and be able to sort of match my... 

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:36.464
My reactions and my communication styles to how&nbsp;that other person is feeling.

00:15:36.464 --> 00:15:44.229
So that you can both&nbsp;"be on the same page" and you can adjust because&nbsp;those things you just couldn't do via text or call.

00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:52.624
Right, so after listening to quite a few of Julia's&nbsp;part three answers, what we notice is that she's&nbsp;really good at developing them.

00:15:52.624 --> 00:15:55.506
She answers the&nbsp;question and then she goes into more detail.

